# Examples that carry a refusal, and how many.
#
# `nts check` **exits 0 when an exported function refuses.** It compiles what it
# can, runs that, compares it with node and reports agreement over the functions
# that survived. So an example can lose most of its cases to a refusal and still
# be counted by `backend_examples` as agreeing -- which is what happened while
# `examples/in-on-a-native-receiver` was being written: with the change under
# test disabled, seven of its nine functions refused, `nts check` said "checked
# 58 cases across 2 function(s), agreed on every case", and exited 0.
#
# A fixture that cannot fail is worth nothing, and the floor above it says 147
# either way. This file is what makes the difference visible: every example that
# refuses anything is listed with its count and the reason, and the step fails on
# an example that is not here or that refuses more than it says.
#
# A count that has gone *down* is progress and prints a note rather than failing;
# edit the number when that happens. A count that has gone up, or a name that is
# not here, is a fixture quietly measuring less than it says it does.
#
# Format: name<space>count<space>why. Blank lines and `#` are ignored.
#
# The count is `nts hir`'s, which is **not** `nts check`'s: `check` prints the
# cascades below a refusal as well, so it says three for `calls` where the
# lowering refuses one thing and two functions fall over behind it. Counting the
# root is what makes the number stable when a cascade lengthens.

# Deliberate: these exist to hold constructs that refuse, and the count is the
# point rather than a debt.
generic-classes-unsupported 1 the constructs the name promises
generator-unsupported 3 the constructs the name promises
dates-unsupported 6 the constructs the name promises
async-unsupported 3 the constructs the name promises
# Was 2 until `86994fb4` landed the mapped-callback arm, which is one of the
# two constructs this example's name promises. The remaining one is a
# `for...of` over an object type.
#
# **Stale from that commit until 2026-09-13, and it printed every run.** The
# step reports a fallen count as progress and passes, deliberately -- a gate
# that goes red when you fix something teaches people to stop fixing things --
# so the only thing standing between the note and the edit is somebody acting
# on a line in a *passing* run. Nobody did, including on the run where this
# same file was audited row by row: that audit compared the third column and
# the second was the one that had moved.
#
# The cost is not tidiness. The comparison is `count > want`, so a `want` left
# at 2 lets a regression back to 2 pass silently -- a blind spot exactly the
# size of the progress, over the code that just changed.
array-from-unsupported 1 the constructs the name promises
mathops-unsupported 4 the constructs the name promises
# **`a-throw-that-stays-in-its-function` carries no row any more**, and that is the whole
# raising row closed: a method's raising copy is reached by name, an overridden member's
# and an interface member's through a slot, an accessor's either way, a `sort` comparator's
# at the uniform entry, and a constructor's by naming `{owner}#constructor@raises`. Its 19
# functions and 551 cases are all answers now, which is a stronger statement than a count
# of expected refusals ever was -- `agree` fails if any of them stops.
# `a-try-around-a-cycle-of-calls` had a row here, one indirection out from the
# example above: the `try` around a call whose *callee* reached a method, naming
# `shared.lifecycle`, the leaf. **It refuses nothing now** -- a method has a
# raising copy -- and the row is gone rather than set to 0, because the step's
# second loop asks for exactly that: a listed name carrying no refusal prints
# `remove it from the table` on every run, and a note printed every run is one
# nobody reads.
#
# Its own note recorded being **missing here from the commit that added the
# example until 2026-09-25**, which is the hazard the `array-from-unsupported`
# note above describes from the other direction: the gate run that cleared that
# commit was taken before the example was in the tree, and this step is the only
# one that would have said so.
# Nine arms, and the one refusal is the **boundary** rather than a debt: `Math[k]`
# where `k` is a variable is a member decided at run time, and reading it as the
# name `k` would put it in the same slot as `Math["k"]`. A count of 0 here means
# that narrowing was lost; a count above 1 means one of the five bracket arms
# stopped lowering. Both directions matter, which is why the arm is in the
# example rather than left out of it.
#
# On a compiler built before `member_access` existed the count is **6** and the
# differential compares 3 functions instead of 8 -- the only way round in which
# this example tests anything.
a-member-read-through-a-bracket 1 a variable key, which is a member decided at run time

# Incidental: an example written for something else that happens to contain a
# construct the compiler refuses. Each is a real gap and each is somebody's next
# item; the number is what stops a second one arriving unnoticed.
# Two exported generics nothing in the file instantiates. They were never
# compiled and the export could never have been filled; what changed on
# 2026-09-11 is that the compiler says so instead of emitting nothing and
# leaving the napi wrapper to report the effect with the cause unsaid.
advanced 2 an exported generic function this program never instantiates
module-evaluation 1 a regular expression literal
calls 1 an erased value where a concrete representation is wanted
# Its `try` returns `handler(await failing(...))`, and `handler` is a function
# value: if it threw, the `catch` beside it would not run. It does not throw here,
# which is why this example passed while the escape was silent -- the refusal
# arrived with the fix and the example is what it cost.
jsx 2 a jsx self closing element
enum-reverse-map-unsupported 2 the constructs the name promises
an-optional-method-called-optionally 2 the two `uncork?(): void;` and `note?(by): void;` declarations on the abstract class, which have no body -- the *calls* are what this fixture is about and they compile
using-unsupported 2 the constructs the name promises
# Two of twelve arms, each a named edge of thenable resolution rather than a
# debt of this fixture. `throwsInThen`: a throw from `then` must reject the
# promise, and a method has no raising copy for the job to catch it through.
# `asItsBase`: the checker types `await held` from `held: Base`, and `Derived`'s
# `then` delivers a number a `Base` slot cannot hold. A count of 0 here means a
# site stopped asking the census, which fulfils with the object where node calls
# its `then`.
an-await-of-a-thenable 2 a `then` that can throw, and a thenable held as a base whose `then` delivers what the base cannot hold
a-narrowed-value-assigned-to-a-const 1 the boundary arm: a narrowed `let` a closure writes to is bound to its cell, so the branch does not rebind it
an-enum-member-read-by-its-name 1 the boundary arm: a reverse mapping at a computed index, which is `undefined` for a value no member has
a-nested-function-used-before-its-declaration 1 the boundary arm: a hoisted closure whose capture is declared inside the block, where node throws on the dead zone and a closure built at the top would answer NaN
# The ordinary erased entries retain these boundaries; the proved number/string
# source copies compile all 18 exported roots. Count roots rather than the
# forwarding cascade below `fixed`, as the table header requires.
a-closed-call-recovers-an-erased-parameter 9 ordinary erased arithmetic and method receivers still refuse; closed number/string copies supply the behavioral roots
a-closed-call-recovers-an-erased-object-parameter 3 the erased originals of `sum`, `measured` and `moved` still refuse a field and two methods on `any`; class-instance copies supply every root
